{% comment %}
  The model's inputs, step by step: one simple figure per step (tools/hatteras_model_steps.py, the 1996-2015 run),
  with Back / Next buttons and numbered dots (assets/hatteras/js/model-steps.js). Without the script every step shows.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign ms = '/assets/hatteras/img/model_steps/' | relative_url %}
<div class="hat-steps" id="model-steps">
  <div class="hat-steps-nav" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="hat-steps-prev" aria-label="Previous step">&larr; Back</button>
    <div class="hat-steps-dots" role="tablist" aria-label="Steps"></div>
    <button type="button" class="hat-steps-next" aria-label="Next step">Next &rarr;</button>
  </div>

  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 1">
    <h3><span>Step 1 of 7</span> From photo to model grid</h3>
    <p>Planes with lasers (lidar) measure the height of the ground every metre. The model uses one height for every 10 m square. Here is one 500 m stretch of the island between Avon and Salvo, from the sound on the left to the ocean on the right: drag the handle to compare the aerial photo with the height maps.</p>
    <div class="hat-ms-compare" data-photo="{{ ms }}step1_photo.jpg" data-lidar="{{ ms }}step1_lidar.png" data-grid="{{ ms }}step1_grid.png">
      <div class="hat-seg hat-ms-layers" role="group" aria-label="Compare the photo with">
        <button type="button" data-layer="grid" class="on">The model's 10 m grid</button>
        <button type="button" data-layer="lidar">Lidar, every 1 m</button>
      </div>
      <div class="hat-compare-stage">
        <div class="hat-compare-photos hat-ms-photos" role="region" aria-label="The aerial photo compared with the height map">
          <img class="hat-ms-before" src="{{ ms }}step1_photo.jpg" alt="Aerial photo, 1996, of a 950 m wide slice of the island from the sound to the ocean" decoding="async">
          <img class="hat-ms-after" src="{{ ms }}step1_grid.png" alt="The same slice as the model's grid of heights, one value every 10 m" decoding="async">
        </div>
        <span class="hat-compare-tag left">Aerial photo, 1996</span>
        <span class="hat-compare-tag right hat-ms-tag">The model's 10 m grid</span>
        <div class="hat-compare-divider hat-ms-divider"><span></span></div>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" class="hat-compare-range hat-ms-range" aria-label="Drag to compare the photo with the height map">
      </div>
      <img class="hat-ms-scale" src="{{ ms }}step1_scale.png" alt="Colour scale: height above high tide in metres, with water in blue" loading="lazy">
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 2">
    <h3><span>Step 2 of 7</span> The whole island</h3>
    <p>The model island is 90 stretches like that one (outlined in yellow), side by side, from Cape Point to just north of Rodanthe. Storms move sand across each stretch; waves move sand along the shore from one stretch to the next. The map is stretched across so all 45 km fit; the island is really 1 to 2 km wide.</p>
    <picture><source media="(max-width: 576px)" srcset="{{ ms }}step2_phone.png"><img src="{{ ms }}step2.png" alt="Map of the height of Hatteras Island from Cape Point to north of Rodanthe at the start of the 1996 run, coloured from low green to high brown and white dunes" loading="lazy"></picture>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 3">
    <h3><span>Step 3 of 7</span> The shape of the coast</h3>
    <p>The model lines its 90 stretches up along a straight line, but the real coast curves: around Cape Point the shoreline bends far inland. So the model also keeps how far each stretch's shoreline sits back from its straight line. That tells it the true shape of the coast, and so the angle at which waves strike each stretch.</p>
    <picture><source media="(max-width: 576px)" srcset="{{ ms }}step_offset_phone.png"><img src="{{ ms }}step_offset.png" alt="Chart of the coastline as the model sees it: the shoreline sits about 6 km landward of the model's straight line at Cape Point and comes close to it near Rodanthe" loading="lazy"></picture>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 4">
    <h3><span>Step 4 of 7</span> The storms that happened</h3>
    <p>Every storm in the record from 1996 to 2015 is sent over the island in the year it happened: 207 of them, from Hurricane Isabel to small nor'easters. Where the water and waves reach higher than the dunes, sand washes over onto the island.</p>
    <picture><source media="(max-width: 576px)" srcset="{{ ms }}step3_phone.png"><img src="{{ ms }}step3.png" alt="Chart of the lowest dune top on each 500 m stretch from Cape Point to just north of Rodanthe, with lines for the water levels of Isabel (5.2 m), Irene and Earl (3.8 m) and a large nor'easter (3.0 m); many stretches dip below the lines, most of all around Rodanthe and between Avon and Salvo" loading="lazy"></picture>
    <p class="hat-step-note">The chart uses each stretch's dunes as they were before any storm. Dunes are worn down while a storm goes on, so water gets over in more places than it shows.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 5">
    <h3><span>Step 5 of 7</span> Waves and rising seas</h3>
    <p>The sea rises 4 mm a year in the run, close to the 3.8 mm a year measured at the Duck tide gauge over the same years. Waves arrive from the north-east and the south-east, a little more often from the north-east, and move sand along the shore.</p>
    <picture><source media="(max-width: 576px)" srcset="{{ ms }}step4_phone.png"><img src="{{ ms }}step4.png" alt="Left: monthly sea level at Duck from 1996 to 2015 with a rising trend line. Right: a sketch of the island with a thicker arrow for waves from the north-east and a thinner one from the south-east" loading="lazy"></picture>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 6">
    <h3><span>Step 6 of 7</span> What people did</h3>
    <p>The model follows the management that really happened. In front of NC-12, dunes are rebuilt after storms and sand is pushed off the road; in the villages, part of the overwash is cleared from streets and yards. The road was moved inland north of Buxton in 1999, and sand was pumped onto the beach at the Rodanthe S-curves in 2014.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step5_road.png" alt="Aerial photo from 2010 of one stretch between Avon and Salvo, with the centre line of NC-12 and the front of the dunes drawn on it, about 200 m apart" loading="lazy">
    <p class="hat-step-note">The model knows how far the road sits behind the dunes on every stretch, so it knows where dunes must be rebuilt to protect it.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 7">
    <h3><span>Step 7 of 7</span> What it is compared with</h3>
    <p>When the run ends, the model's shoreline is compared with what satellites measured: how fast the shoreline moved on each stretch from 1996 to 2015. The closer the two match, the more confidence there is in using the model to explore what might happen next.</p>
    <picture><source media="(max-width: 576px)" srcset="{{ ms }}step6_phone.png"><img src="{{ ms }}step6.png" alt="Bar chart along the island of measured shoreline change from 1996 to 2015: blue bars growing near Cape Point and north of Avon, red bars eroding at Buxton, south of Avon and Rodanthe" loading="lazy"></picture>
  </section>
  <p class="hat-caption hat-numbered">The inputs of the 1996–2015 run, step by step, drawn from the same files the model reads. Heights are metres above mean high water. Storms: the peak water level at the shore (tide, surge and wave run-up). Satellite shorelines: CoastSat.</p>
</div>
<script defer src="{{ '/assets/hatteras/js/model-steps.js' | relative_url }}"></script>
